🎯 OVERALL GOAL (4 weeks)
By end of week 4, you will have:
• A working detection lab (Elastic + endpoint telemetry)
• 3–5 detection rules (Sigma → working queries)
• A basic test harness (attack simulation + validation)
• 1 hunting playbook
• 1 brown-bag session delivered
⸻
⚙️ WEEK 1 — Build the Lab (Foundation)
Outcome: You can see, query, and understand telemetry
⸻
Day 1–2: Environment Setup
Choose simple stack (don’t over-engineer):
• SIEM: Elastic (recommended) OR Splunk trial
• Endpoint:
• Windows VM + Sysmon
• Linux VM (optional but good)
Tasks
• Install Elastic stack (Docker or local)
• Install agent on Windows VM
• Install Sysmon with a good config (SwiftOnSecurity config)
👉 Deliverable:
• Screenshot of logs flowing (process creation, network logs)
⸻
Day 3–4: Telemetry Understanding
You are NOT allowed to blindly write detections.
Tasks
• Generate activity:
• Open apps
• Run PowerShell commands
• Download files
• Observe logs:
• Process creation
• Parent-child relationships
• Command line arguments
👉 Deliverable:
• Document:
• “Top 10 useful fields for detection”
• Example: [process.name](http://process.name/), [parent.process.name](http://parent.process.name/), command_line
⸻
Day 5–6: ATT&CK Mapping (Critical thinking starts)
Pick 5 techniques from MITRE ATT&CK
Example:
• T1059 – Command Execution
• T1003 – Credential Dumping
Tasks
For each technique:
• What does it look like in logs?
• What tools simulate it?
• What signals exist?
👉 Deliverable:
• 1-page per technique:
• Behavior
• Log evidence
• Detection idea
⸻
Day 7: Mini Review
Ask yourself:
• Can I explain logs without guessing?
• Can I map behavior → telemetry?
If not, repeat.
⸻
⚙️ WEEK 2 — Detection Engineering (Build real signals)
Outcome: You write detections that actually work
⸻
Day 1–2: Sigma Rules (Core skill)
Learn Sigma structure.
Tasks
• Write 3 Sigma rules:
1. Suspicious PowerShell execution
2. Encoded command usage
3. Office spawning cmd.exe
👉 Deliverable:
• 3 working Sigma rules (even basic is fine)
⸻
Day 3–4: Convert to SIEM Queries
• Convert Sigma → Elastic query (KQL/EQL)
Tasks
• Run queries in your lab
• Validate they trigger correctly
👉 Deliverable:
• Working queries + screenshots
⸻
Day 5–6: False Positive Analysis (THIS is where engineers stand out)
• Run normal activity
• See where rules break
Tasks
• Tune rules:
• Add exclusions
• Refine conditions
👉 Deliverable:
• Before vs After:
• FP rate reduced (even qualitatively)
⸻
Day 7: Documentation
Write like a professional:
Each rule should include:
• What it detects
• Why it matters
• Known false positives
• Tuning logic
⸻
⚙️ WEEK 3 — Testing Blueprint + Threat Simulation
Outcome: You stop guessing and start validating detections scientifically
⸻
Day 1–2: Attack Simulation Setup
Use:
• Atomic Red Team (lightweight) OR
• Manual commands
Simulate:
• PowerShell abuse
• Credential dumping (safe simulation)
• Suspicious process chains
⸻
Day 3–4: Build Test Harness (your edge)
This is where most people fail — you won’t.
Structure:
Test Case:
- Technique: T1059
- Command executed
- Expected logs
- Detection triggered? (Y/N)
Tasks
• Create 5 test cases
• Run them
• Record results
👉 Deliverable:
• Detection validation sheet
⸻
Day 5–6: Gap Analysis
Now think like a hunter:
• What DID NOT trigger?
• Why?
Tasks
• Improve at least 1 detection
• Add 1 new detection based on gap
⸻
Day 7: Write Hunting Hypothesis
Example: “If attackers use encoded PowerShell, we should see abnormal parent-child relationships”
👉 Deliverable:
• 1 hunt hypothesis + query
⸻
⚙️ WEEK 4 — Hunting + Brown Bag (Make yourself visible)
Outcome: You transition from learner → operator
⸻
Day 1–2: Run Hunt
Use your hypothesis
Tasks
• Query data
• Investigate anomalies
• Document findings
👉 Deliverable:
• 1 hunting report:
• Hypothesis
• Query
• Findings
• Conclusion
⸻
Day 3–4: Build Automation (small but powerful)
Leverage your AI strength here.
Tasks
• Create simple script/playbook:
• Input: alert
• Output: enriched context (process tree, hash, etc.)
👉 Deliverable:
• Working enrichment script (Python or your platform)
⸻
Day 5: Prepare Brown Bag
Title: “From Alerts to Detection Engineering: Building High-Quality Detections”
Structure:
1. Problem:
• Too many alerts, low signal
2. What I built:
• Lab
• Detections
• Testing
3. Demo:
• Show detection firing
4. Key learnings:
• False positives matter more than detection logic
5. Impact:
• Reduced noise
• Better detection confidence
⸻
Day 6: Deliver It
• Keep it 20–30 mins
• Be sharp, not fancy
⸻
Day 7: Reflection
Ask:
• What did I build?
• What can I improve?
• What would I do differently?
